A||||13||Cooter||3.4 v6, So far I found. . .||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||04-01-2000||09:32 PM||||I have had my 3.4 in the garage for a few weeks now and so far I have learned a few things. <BR>  A "normal rotation" water pump will not fit the 3.4's timing cover.<BR>  Even though it looks like it would fit, a distributer will not fit with the 3.4's intake. It requires a little notching and creative tig welding- pic's will follow soon!<BR>  It is a pain to try to get a fiero's vac. operated EGR to work on an engine with a totally electronic EGR. I found the solution in a 86 camaro v6 (hopefully)<BR>  A Fiero 2.8 has a larger throttle blade than that on the 3.4! This suprised me.<BR>  The lower (or the lowest) section of the intake manifold is the same for a lot of iron head 60 degree v6's. No need to change this part out when putting on the Fiero intake. By leaving this in place, you do not have to pull rocker arms and mess up the valve adjustment.<BR>  The fuel rail and injectors are a pain in the butt to get out. If you have a easy way to do this, please let me know.<BR>  An automatic flywheel for the 3.4 is 1/2 inch larger than the replacement manual tranny flywheel from the 3.4 TDC. If you are drilling your own starter mount holes, BE SURE to use the flywheel that you will be running with the engine. Dont assume that the one on your used engine is the same size as what you will need.<BR>  An automatic equiped 3.4 will need to have the pilot shaft bushing installed in the crankshaft.<BR>  This is all that I could think of for now. More to come later if it is important.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||04-02-2000||12:59 PM||||Cooter,<P>Great info. I'm collecting this kind of stuff for my 3.4 rebuild so anything you find, please pass it along. I do have a question about your statement:<P>"The lower (or the lowest) section of the intake manifold is the same for a lot of iron head 60 degree v6's. No need to change this part out when putting on the Fiero intake. "<P>Are you stating that the 3.4 heads are the same configuration as the Fiero's 2.8 or are you saying the 3.4 came with aluminum heads and you can use the upper Fiero intake plenum if you use the lowers from the 3.4? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Thanks,<BR>Roy<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Cooter||04-02-2000||01:09 PM||||The iron head 3.4 has the same head configuration as the 2.8<BR>The 2.8 heads would bolt onto a 3.4 or vice-versa. I dont know about 3.4 aluminum heads but I am sure that those would be different.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||04-02-2000||09:43 PM||||Half-way there...sort of. I got one of the starter bolts drilled and tapped tonight. The final bit was a 21/64 (.328")which worked out a little "tight" compared to the suggested .339" I just could not find a size "R" drill bit to use with the 10x1.50 tap. To keep the bolt going straight I used a framer's square and drilled a little at a time and then made sure the bit was still straight again. It took a while. For the outer hole, I am going to cheat and use a wood worker's drill guide used for drilling dowel holes. With the starter mounted, the drill guide will fit in the outer bolt hole and keep the bit from walking (hopefully). I have already turned the engine a round or two with the starter the way it is to make sure it sounded "right" Lucky for me it does! When I am finished with the drill bit, drill guide and tap they are going to be given to whoever wants them because I AM NEVER DOING THIS AGAIN!!!!!! Thank you.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Dcp00807.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Dcp00808.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||04-03-2000||10:05 AM||||I'm interested!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Keep us posted on your progress. It sounds like your going about it the right way (or at least the way I can afford).<P>Thanks for the pics,<BR>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000005||87PegasusGT||04-03-2000||03:19 PM||||So do I use 3.4 flywheel for a 2.8 manual? Or one from an 88 GT / 3.4 DTC?<BR>Also, computer from Fiero or Camaro?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||04-03-2000||05:58 PM||||I bought a new flywheel, but I could have used the old one from my 88 2.8- I decided to get a new one because the old flywheel has 180,000 miles on it and is probably a little stressed. The 3.4 and 88 up 2.8's are internally balanced and have a neutral balanced flywheel. The flywheel I bought is from a 93 Lumina and costed $88 instead of $250 for a new 88 Fiero one. I will be using the fiero computer with the 3.4 injectors.||||reg||1
Z||000007||87PegasusGT||04-03-2000||06:37 PM||||Thanks.<BR>BTW, would you use 3.4 chip or 2.8 Hiperformance chip (ADS or similar?)||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||04-03-2000||10:22 PM||||Cooter,is the early Fiero flywheel the same,other than the balance difference,as the'88 one?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||04-03-2000||10:37 PM||||Those are good questions. I dont know the answers to either one of them. Sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||04-03-2000||11:23 PM||||Are the flywheels the same diameter,or,do the '88s use the same starter as the early cars?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||04-03-2000||11:35 PM||||The 87 and down Fiero flywheels are externally balanced.  sfaik they are the same, other than that.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Capt Fiero||04-04-2000||10:55 PM||||I have not done this before but from what I have been told and the way that I plan on doing it is, you can take your pre 88 flywheel and either have someone else or you can do it yourself, have the weight removed. It is simialar to a tire weight that is welded to the flywheel.  And is not hard to remove.<BR>David||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||04-05-2000||08:30 AM||||The weight is cast into the flywheel and must be machined off. Unless you have a friend with a lathe expect about $100||||reg||1
